Jukka Lahtinen wrote on 09/20/2003 06:53 PM:
> Pasi Kallinen <pkalli@cs.joensuu.fi> writes:
>> Jukka Lahtinen <jukkal@despammed.com> wrote:
> 
>> > if [ "$TERM" == xterm  -a  -f $HOME/.nethackrc.x11 ] ; then
>> >      NETHACKOPTIONS=$HOME/.nethackrc.x11
>> > fi
> 
>> Wouldn't it be sensible to check that $DISPLAY is set, too?
> 
> I guess it would be a good idea. 
> But why isn't the "$TERM" == xterm test enough? Does something set $TERM
> to xterm even outside of X11?

No, it's not as I might have DISPLAY set (even in a console) for various
reasons. It doesn't mean that I want to run nethack on that DISPLAY or
in some other mode, just because I have set DISPLAY. My desire to do so
I can express in the config-file and everything else is uninformed
guesswork on the script's part as it cannot know what's up in the user's
head and/or system.

OTOH, TERM should be grepped for xterm IHMO, not ==-test(1)ed, if this
is employed at all. It seems to me that it's simplest and best to have
two or more USER-AUTHORED shell-scripts (or one smart script which
accepts options and/or uses basename and is linked to) for each mode of
operation, setting appropriate NETHACKOPTIONS=@files, isn't it? Trying
to make it too easy for the user by making the nethack command smart
out-of-the-box could lead to a microsoftish "I, the program, know better
than YOU" feel. Furthermore, "normal" users desire only one mode of
operation anyway and "advanced" users can be expected to write that
easy, short shellscript, applescript, batch-file, whatever, specific for
their needs.

